From: Joan Karadimov <joan.karadimov@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] Make isOctoToolRunning() answer which tool is running

0006 made this return true unconditionally on Windows. That answered the
only caller I had looked at -- main()'s "am I the installed octopi?", a gate
that exists so the setuid-root helper can trust its parent and that has
nothing left to protect once the helper is not built.

It got the other caller wrong. isNotifierBusy() opens with

    if (!UnixCommand::isOctoToolRunning("octopi-notifier")) return res;

and is asking whether the *notifier* is the program running. On Linux that
is always false when asked from inside octopi -- the test compares this
executable's path against /usr/bin/octopi-notifier -- so the guard always
fires and everything below it is dead code upstream. Returning true stopped
the guard firing, and what is below it does not terminate: see 0012.

Comparing the executable's own name answers both questions. octopi asking
about octopi-notifier gets false; main() asking about octopi gets true.

Not for upstream: it is the Windows branch of a check that means something
else where / is /.
diff --git a/src/unixcommand.cpp b/src/unixcommand.cpp
index 8978971..7368357 100644
--- a/src/unixcommand.cpp
+++ b/src/unixcommand.cpp
@@ -1283,13 +1283,19 @@ bool UnixCommand::isAppRunning(const QString &appName, bool justOneInstance)
 bool UnixCommand::isOctoToolRunning(const QString &octoToolName)
 {
 #ifdef Q_OS_WIN
-  // This gate exists so that the setuid-root helper can trust the parent that
-  // invoked it -- hence octphelper's own "Suspicious execution method". That
-  // helper is not built here (see the top-level CMakeLists), so there is
-  // nothing left for the gate to protect, and /usr/bin is not where a mingw
-  // package installs anything.
-  Q_UNUSED(octoToolName)
-  return true;
+  // Two callers ask two different questions here. main() asks "am I the
+  // installed octopi?" -- a gate that exists so the setuid-root helper can
+  // trust its parent, and that has nothing to protect once the helper is not
+  // built. isNotifierBusy() asks "is the notifier the program running?", and
+  // that one must answer no when it is not, or it goes on to interrogate a
+  // notifier that was never there.
+  //
+  // Answering the first question with an unconditional true got the second
+  // one wrong and hung octopi on startup. Compare the name instead, which
+  // answers both: /usr/bin is not where a mingw package installs anything,
+  // but the executable is still named after the tool it is.
+  return QFileInfo(QCoreApplication::applicationFilePath()).baseName()
+         == octoToolName;
 #else
   // QCoreApplication answers this without /proc, and gives the same path
   // readlink("/proc/self/exe") did.
